        <div class="main-content">
            <h2><u>Highways</u></h2>
            <p>A highway is a major public road designed for high-speed travel between cities and towns, often featuring multiple lanes, limited access points like on-ramps, and a barrier separating traffic going in opposite directions. Highways are essential for efficient transportation, connecting distant areas and allowing for a large volume of traffic to flow quickly and safely.</p>
            <h3>Key characteristics</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Multiple lanes: Highways typically have several lanes in each direction to accommodate high traffic volume.</li>
                <li>Limited access: Access to highways is usually restricted to on-ramps and off-ramps, which are designed to allow vehicles to enter and exit without crossing oncoming traffic streams.</li>
                <li>Divided roadway: Many highways are divided by a physical barrier, such as a median strip, to separate traffic traveling in opposite directions.</li>
                <li>High-speed design: They are built to allow for high-speed travel, with features like gentle curves and long sight lines to improve safety.</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>Types of highways</h3>
            <ol>
                <li>Expressways/Freeways: These are a type of highway designed for high-speed traffic with limited access.</li>
                <li>Interstate Highways: This refers to a specific system of highways in the United States that links major cities across the 48 contiguous states.</li>
                <li>Ring Roads/Beltways: These highways encircle an urban area, allowing traffic to bypass the city center.</li>
                <li>Toll Roads/Turnpikes: These are highways where drivers pay a toll to use them.</li>
            </ol>
